[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Object-Relational Mapping (ORM) in PHP: A Technical Tutorial"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Object-Relational Mapping (ORM) is a programming technique that converts data between incompatible type systems using object-oriented programming languages. This allows you to query and manipulate data from a database using an object-oriented paradigm."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Why Use an ORM?"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Increased Productivity: ORMs abstract away the complexities of database interactions, allowing developers to focus on application logic."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Improved Code Readability: ORM code is generally more readable and maintainable than raw SQL queries."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Database Portability: ORMs can often be configured to work with different database systems with minimal code changes."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Security: ORMs often provide built-in protection against SQL injection attacks."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Popular PHP ORMs"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Doctrine: A powerful and feature-rich ORM, often used in Symfony projects."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Eloquent: The ORM included with the Laravel framework, known for its ease of use."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Propel: Another popular ORM for PHP, offering code generation capabilities."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Example using Eloquent (Laravel)"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Let's illustrate a basic example using Eloquent, the ORM that comes with Laravel. First, ensure you have a Laravel project set up and configured with your database."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"// Create a model (e.g., 'User.php')
namespace App\Models;

use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Model;

class User extends Model
{
    // Table name (optional, defaults to lowercase plural of class name)
    protected $table = 'users';

    // Primary key (optional, defaults to 'id')
    protected $primaryKey = 'id';

    // Indicates if the model should be timestamped (created_at, updated_at)
    public $timestamps = true;

    // Fillable attributes (attributes that can be mass-assigned)
    protected $fillable = ['name', 'email', 'password'];

    // Hidden attributes (attributes that should not be visible in arrays or JSON)
    protected $hidden = ['password', 'remember_token'];
}
"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"This code defines a 'User' model that corresponds to a 'users' table in your database. The '$fillable' array specifies which attributes can be mass-assigned (e.g., when creating a new user)."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"// Using the model to interact with the database

// Create a new user
$user = new App\Models\User();
$user->name = 'John Doe';
$user->email = 'john.doe@example.com';
$user->password = bcrypt('secret'); // Hash the password
$user->save();

// Retrieve a user by ID
$user = App\Models\User::find(1);

echo $user->name; // Output: John Doe

// Retrieve all users
$users = App\Models\User::all();

foreach ($users as $user) {
    echo $user->email . "\n";
}

// Update a user
$user = App\Models\User::find(1);
$user->email = 'new.email@example.com';
$user->save();

// Delete a user
$user = App\Models\User::find(1);
$user->delete();"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"This code demonstrates basic CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) operations using the 'User' model.  Note the use of 'bcrypt()' for password hashing, a crucial security practice."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Relationships"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"ORMs excel at managing relationships between database tables. Eloquent provides several relationship types, including one-to-one, one-to-many, and many-to-many."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"// Example: One-to-many relationship (User has many Posts)

// In the User model:
namespace App\Models;

use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Model;

class User extends Model
{
    public function posts()
    {
        return $this->hasMany('App\Models\Post');
    }
}

// In the Post model:
namespace App\Models;

use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Model;

class Post extends Model
{
    public function user()
    {
        return $this->belongsTo('App\Models\User');
    }
}

// Usage:
$user = App\Models\User::find(1);

foreach ($user->posts as $post) {
    echo $post->title . "\n";
}

$post = App\Models\Post::find(1);

echo $post->user->name; // Access the user's name through the relationship"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example defines a one-to-many relationship between 'User' and 'Post' models. A user can have multiple posts, and each post belongs to one user. The 'hasMany()' and 'belongsTo()' methods define these relationships."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"ORMs significantly simplify database interactions in PHP applications. By abstracting away the complexities of raw SQL, they improve developer productivity, code readability, and maintainability. While this tutorial focused on Eloquent, the concepts apply broadly to other PHP ORMs like Doctrine and Propel. Choose the ORM that best suits your project's needs and your personal preferences."}]
